            <ArticleTitle>The quest for lower alcoholic wines.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>238-241</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/1751-7915.12594</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Wine industry is engaged in finding technological ways to decrease alcohol concentration in wines without spoiling their organoleptic properties. Such challenge requires, among other strategies, modification of the yeast strains carrying out the fermentation. In this issue of Microb. Biotechnol., Goold and colleagues have reviewed one of the most straightforward yeast modification, altering its metabolism to produce glycerol instead of alcohol.</AbstractText>
